  "query_preview": "..\nSunday: An upper level low currently centered over the Washington-\nOregon border has brought cooler temperatures, rain, thunderstorms, \nand gusty winds to the forecast area. Showers regionwide have \nlargely diminished.  Most showers have diminished, some linger in \ncentral and northern central Washington, as well as in the Idaho \nPanhandle. A couple high resolution models have some showers moving \nthrough overnight. Given that the vorticity maximum is moving \neastward and out of the area with the center of the low, chances for \nconvection continuing into the night are low. Rather, main potential \nimpacts with any showers will be heavy isolated rainfall, since the \nmotion with most of these showers is either very slow or nearly \nstationary. As the low moves further east, a deformation band will \nwrap around the low, bringing showers that will primarily impact the \nNorthern Idaho Panhandle. Precipitation estimates show much of the \npanhandle getting 0.25 inches of rain or more through this evening. \n\nAlongside the rain, a surface low strengthening over Montana will \ncreate a strong pressure gradient with the high off the PNW coast. \nCurrently, models are showing a 10-15mb gradient between SEA and COE \nSunday night into Monday. As is typical with an east-west oriented \npressure gradient, the Cascade gaps are expected to see the \nstrongest wind gusts, with gusts 35-40 expected and isolated higher \nwind gusts 45-50 mph. This will bring the potential for blowing \nobjects, minor tree damage, and challenging driving conditions,\nparticularly for high profile vehicles. \n\nMonday through Tuesday: Bands of rain will continue to wrap around \nthe low and move into the area through Monday. Overall precipitation \ntotals show the Spokane/CdA area down to the Pullman area receiving \n0.25-0.40 inches of precipitation through Monday afternoon. \nHighest rainfall totals will be in the Idaho Panhandle and \nparticularly in Shoshone County, which could see rain totals of \nan inch or more. Outside of these showers, maximum temperatures \nacross the Inland Northwest remain lower than normal by 5-10 \ndegrees and will remain in the low 70s to low 80s. The Palouse \nand ID Panhandle will be the coolest areas on Monday with highs\nin the 60s which is around 15 degrees below normal. Minimum \ntemperatures also remain below normal in the low 40s to low 50s.\n\nWednesday through Friday: The middle to the end of the work week \nwill be relatively dry apart from showers in the mountains due to \norographic lift as lower heights remain over the area. Temperatures \nwill continue to be cooler than normal, though a weak ridge moving \ncloser to the coast near the end of the week will allow for some \nwarming. Clusters near the holiday weekend begin showing signals for \nsaid ridge strengthening and moving through the forecast area, \nsupporting a warmup. /AS",
